-- title:
--   Upper-triangular twisted conjugators for unipotent-type norm class
-- statement:
--   Let $L/F$ be a finite Galois extension of fields and let $\sigma$ be an $F$-automorphism of $L$ such that every $F$-automorphism of $L$ lies in the subgroup generated by the integer powers of $\sigma$ (so $\mathrm{Gal}(L/F)$ is cyclic with generator $\sigma$). Let $\beta \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L)$ have vanishing lower-left entry, $\beta_{1,0} = 0$, and let $\gamma \in \mathrm{GL}_2(F)$ lie in [`AutomorphicForm.unipotentCell F`](def/AutomorphicForm_GL2ConjugacyCells.html#L29), that is, the underlying matrix of $\gamma$ does not satisfy the predicate `IsCentralType` and its characteristic polynomial is $(X - C\,a)^2$ for some $a \in F$. Assume that the norm-class map [`LT.TwistedNorm.normClassMap`](def/TwistedNormClasses.html#L766) attached to $\sigma$ sends the $\sigma$-conjugacy class of $\beta$ — the class of $\beta$ for the relation identifying $\delta_1$ with $\delta_2$ when $\delta_2 = k^{-1}\delta_1\,\sigma(k)$ for some $k \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L)$, $\sigma$ acting entrywise — to the $\mathrm{GL}_2(F)$-conjugacy class of $\gamma$. Then for every $\eta \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L)$ such that the lower-left entry of $\eta^{-1}\beta\,\sigma(\eta)$ vanishes, the lower-left entry of $\eta$ vanishes as well.
--
--   The statement says that an upper-triangular $\beta$ whose twisted norm class is the conjugacy class of an element of unipotent type can be $\sigma$-conjugated into the Borel subgroup of upper-triangular matrices only by elements of that Borel subgroup, so that the twisted conjugator is determined up to the Borel. It is used in the analysis of the unipotent-type $\sigma$-conjugacy classes of $\mathrm{GL}_2(L)$, being cited in the characterisation of which elements admit an upper-triangular twisted representative with nonzero trace condition.
